MinIO Setup Guide for Windows\par
\par
1. Download MinIO\par
Go to: https://min.io/download\par
\par
2. Create local data directory\par
mkdir C:\\minio\\data\par
\par
3. Set MinIO credentials\par
setx MINIO_ROOT_USER minioadmin\par
setx MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD minioadmin\par
\par
4. Start MinIO\par
C:\\minio\\minio.exe server C:\\minio\\data --console-address ":9001"\par
\par
5. Open MinIO console\par
http://localhost:9001\par
\par
Login:\par
Username: minioadmin\par
Password: minioadmin\par
\par
6. Create bucket\par
Create a bucket named:\par
loghawk\par
\par
7. Optional: create a folder inside the bucket\par
You can use:\par
loghawk/logs\par
\par
8. Upload local files to MinIO\par
import os\par
import boto3\par
\par
s3 = boto3.client(\par
    "s3",\par
    endpoint_url="http://localhost:9000",\par
    aws_access_key_id="minioadmin",\par
    aws_secret_access_key="minioadmin",\par
    region_name="us-east-1"\par
)\par
\par
bucket = "loghawk"\par
local_dir = r"C:\\mywork\\loghawk\\data\\logs"\par
\par
for root, _, files in os.walk(local_dir):\par
    for file in files:\par
        local_path = os.path.join(root, file)\par
        key = os.path.relpath(local_path, local_dir).replace("\\\\", "/")\par
        s3.upload_file(local_path, bucket, f"logs/\{key\}")\par
        print(f"Uploaded: \{local_path\} -> logs/\{key\}")\par
\par
9. List objects in the bucket\par
import boto3\par
\par
s3 = boto3.client(\par
    "s3",\par
    endpoint_url="http://localhost:9000",\par
    aws_access_key_id="minioadmin",\par
    aws_secret_access_key="minioadmin",\par
    region_name="us-east-1"\par
)\par
\par
response = s3.list_objects_v2(Bucket="loghawk", Prefix="logs/")\par
for item in response.get("Contents", []):\par
    print(item["Key"])\par
\par
10. Read a file from MinIO\par
import boto3\par
\par
s3 = boto3.client(\par
    "s3",\par
    endpoint_url="http://localhost:9000",\par
    aws_access_key_id="minioadmin",\par
    aws_secret_access_key="minioadmin",\par
    region_name="us-east-1"\par
)\par
\par
obj = s3.get_object(Bucket="loghawk", Key="logs/sample.json")\par
print(obj["Body"].read().decode("utf-8"))\par
\par
11. S3 URI style\par
MinIO is S3-compatible, so you can think in S3 paths like:\par
s3://loghawk/logs/sample.json\par
\par
Your client still needs:\par
- endpoint URL\par
- access key\par
- secret key\par
- bucket name\par
\par
12. Spark with MinIO\par
from pyspark.sql import SparkSession\par
\par
spark = (\par
    SparkSession.builder\par
    .appName("MinIO-Spark-Example")\par
    .config("spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.endpoint", "http://localhost:9000")\par
    .config("spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.access.key", "minioadmin")\par
    .config("spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.secret.key", "minioadmin")\par
    .config("spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.path.style.access", "true")\par
    .config("spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.impl", "org.apache.hadoop.fs.s3a.S3AFileSystem")\par
    .config("spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.connection.ssl.enabled", "false")\par
    .getOrCreate()\par
)\par
\par
df = spark.read.json("s3a://loghawk/logs/")\par
df.show()\par
\par
13. Notes\par
MinIO is not a Windows folder.\par
It is an S3-compatible object store.\par
Local files stay in: C:\\mywork\\loghawk\\data\\logs\par
MinIO is used for S3-style storage and access.\par
\par
End of guide.\par
